      "Considerations for Implementation\nThe chief audit executive should explain the characteristics of an effective internal audit\nfunction, drawing on the Standards, applicable requirements, and relevant leading practices.\nMandate discussions should clarify:\n     Authority: Direct access to the board and access to organizational records, personnel,\n         activities, and property necessary for audit responsibilities.\n     Role: Internal audit services and any additional responsibilities assigned to the chief audit\n         executive, with consideration of independence safeguards.\n     Responsibilities: Accountability for audit performance, communications, compliance,\n         and conformance with the Standards.\n     Scope: The organizational activities, entities, locations, or other boundaries covered.\n     Services: Assurance, advisory, or more specifically defined services within the mandate.\nChanges that may warrant reconsideration include significant reorganizations, acquisitions,\nleadership changes, revised strategies, changing risks, and changes to relevant laws or\nprofessional standards.\nThe implementation guidance recommends formally considering such changes at least annually,\nwhile recognizing that significant changes can arise at any time."
