        "text": "Requirements — Chief Audit Executive\nThe CAE must provide the board with the information it needs to oversee internal audit. This\nincludes information specifically requested by the board and information the CAE considers\nimportant for effective oversight.\nThe CAE must report to the board and senior management on:\n     The internal audit plan, budget, and significant revisions.\n     Changes that could affect the internal audit mandate or charter.\n     Potential impairments to independence.\n     Results of internal audit services, including conclusions, themes, assurance, advice,\n         insights, and monitoring results.\n     Results of the quality assurance and improvement program.\nWhen disagreements with senior management or other stakeholders affect internal audit’s ability\nto fulfill its responsibilities, the CAE must communicate the relevant facts and circumstances to\nthe board. Examples include disagreements about engagement scope, findings, or other\nsignificant engagement matters.\nEssential Conditions — Board\nThe board should:\n     Communicate with the CAE to understand how internal audit fulfills its mandate.\n     Share its perspective on organizational strategies, objectives, and risks.\n     Establish expectations for the frequency and nature of communications.\n     Define criteria and processes for escalating significant matters.\n     Use internal audit results and management discussions to understand the effectiveness of\n         governance, risk management, and control.\n     Discuss significant disagreements and support the CAE when intervention is appropriate.\nEssential Conditions — Senior Management\nSenior management should:\n     Share its perspective on strategies, objectives, and risks to inform internal audit priorities.\n     Help the board understand the effectiveness of governance, risk management, and\n         control.\n     Work with the board and CAE to establish an appropriate escalation process.\nConsiderations for Implementation\nCommunication should be two-way and may include reports, presentations, formal meetings, and\ninformal discussions.\nThe CAE should periodically confirm that reporting frequency, content, and level of detail meet\nthe board’s needs. Significant matters may require communication between scheduled meetings.\nEscalation criteria should reflect risk tolerance and cover financial and nonfinancial concerns,\nsuch as cybersecurity, strategic initiatives, safety, operational resilience, and reputation.\nDiscussing disagreements with management helps establish accurate facts and fairly present\nmanagement’s perspective. It should not prevent necessary escalation.\nQuarterly communication is an implementation consideration associated with regular board\nmeetings; it is not a universal mandatory frequency under this standard.\nExamples of Evidence of Conformance\n     Board agendas and minutes documenting communications with the CAE.\n     Presentations and reports submitted to the board.\n     Records of communications between scheduled meetings.\n     Documented escalation criteria and procedures.\n     Records showing how significant disagreements were communicated and addressed."
