        "text": "Requirements — Chief Audit Executive\nThe CAE must develop an external quality assessment plan and discuss it with the board.\nAn external quality assessment must be performed:\n     At least once every five years.\n     By a qualified, independent assessor or assessment team.\nA self-assessment with independent validation may be used as an alternative.\nWhen selecting the assessor or team, the CAE must ensure that at least one person holds an\nactive Certified Internal Auditor (CIA) designation.\nEssential Conditions — Board\nThe board should:\n     Discuss plans for assessment by a qualified, independent assessor.\n     Work with senior management and the CAE to determine scope and frequency.\n     Consider charter responsibilities and applicable regulatory requirements when\n         determining scope.\n     Review and approve the assessment plan.\nThe approved plan should address, at minimum:\n     Scope and frequency.\n     Assessor or team competence and independence.\n     The rationale for using self-assessment with independent validation, if selected.\nThe board should also:\n     Require the complete results directly from the assessor, including independent validation\n         results.\n     Approve action plans addressing deficiencies and improvement opportunities.\n     Approve completion timelines and monitor progress.\nEssential Conditions — Senior Management\nSenior management should:\n     Collaborate with the board and CAE on assessment scope and frequency.\n     Review results.\n     Agree appropriate action plans and timelines with the board and CAE.\nConsiderations for Implementation\nMore frequent assessment may be appropriate following significant leadership changes, changes\nin audit methodologies, consolidation of audit functions, high staff turnover, or relevant\nregulatory developments.\nAssessment scope may cover:\n     Conformance with the Standards.\n     Mandate, charter, strategy, methodologies, and audit planning.\n     Applicable legal and regulatory requirements.\n     Performance measures and results.\n     Competence and due professional care.\n     CAE qualifications and role expectations.\n     Organizational positioning and independence.\n     Contribution to governance, risk management, control, and organizational objectives.\n     Stakeholder expectations.\nAssessor Qualifications and Independence\nRelevant assessor experience may include knowledge of the Standards, senior internal audit\nleadership, industry experience, previous external assessments, and recognized external quality\nassessment training.\nIndependence evaluation should consider past, present, and anticipated relationships with the\norganization.\nPotential impairments include:\n     Providing external financial statement audit services.\n     Providing support or advisory services to the internal audit function.\n     Personal relationships.\n     Previous or planned involvement in internal quality assessments.\n     Other advisory relationships affecting objectivity.\nIndividuals from another department within the same organization are not independent for this\npurpose. Related organizations, such as parents or affiliates, may also fail the independence\nrequirement.\nReciprocal assessments between two organizations are not independent. Rotating assessments\namong three or more peer organizations may be independent, but require evaluation; the\nnumber of participants alone does not establish independence.\nSelf-Assessment with Independent Validation\nThis approach typically includes:\n     A comprehensive, documented self-assessment.\n     Independent validation by a qualified external assessor or team.\n     Evaluation of whether the self-assessment was complete and accurate.\n     Benchmarking and interviews with relevant stakeholders.\nExamples of Evidence of Conformance\n     Board minutes approving the assessment plan.\n     Formal external assessment or validation report.\n     Assessor presentations of complete results directly to the board.\n     CAE presentations on results and proposed actions.\n     Board-approved action plans and timelines.\n     Records monitoring implementation."
