    "summary": "The CAE must understand the organization’s governance, risk management, and control processes to develop an effective internal audit strategy and plan.",
    "requirement": "Requirements\nThe CAE must understand the organization’s governance, risk management, and control\nprocesses to develop an effective internal audit strategy and plan.\nIn understanding governance, the CAE must consider how the organization:\n     Establishes strategic objectives and makes strategic and operational decisions.\n     Oversees risk management and control.\n     Promotes an ethical culture.\n     Implements performance management and accountability.\n     Structures its management and operating functions.\n     Communicates risk and control information.\n     Coordinates activities and communications among the board, management, and internal\n         and external assurance providers.\nIn understanding risk management and control, the CAE must consider how the organization\nidentifies and assesses significant risks and selects appropriate controls.\nThis includes risks affecting:\n     Reliability and integrity of financial and operational information.\n     Effectiveness and efficiency of operations and programs.\n     Safeguarding of assets.\n     Compliance with laws and regulations.",
    "implementation": [
      "Considerations for Implementation\nThe CAE should gather information from multiple sources, including board and management\ndiscussions, meeting minutes, prior audit work, and reports from other assurance and advisory\nproviders.\nUnderstanding governance includes examining decision-making structures, committee\nresponsibilities, accountability arrangements, ethical expectations, and the maturity of\ngovernance processes.\nUnderstanding risk management includes considering risk appetite, risk tolerance, risk\nmanagement frameworks, recent risk assessments, and the perspectives of the board and\nmanagement.\nUnderstanding control includes identifying key controls linked to organizational objectives and\nconsidering available evidence about their design and operation.\nAn organizationwide risk and control matrix can help connect:\n     Organizational objectives.\n     Significant risks.\n     Key controls.\n     Existing assessments of control design and operating effectiveness.\nThe understanding developed should inform actual audit priorities and strategic choices."
    ],
    "conformance": [
      "Examples of Evidence of Conformance\n     Documented analysis of governance, risk management, and control processes.\n     Reviewed board and committee charters.\n     Notes from discussions with governance and risk management personnel.\n     Reviews of board minutes and relevant regulatory communications.\n     Analysis of risk appetite and risk tolerance.\n     Reviewed business strategies and plans.\n     Staff orientation or training records.\n     Risk and control matrices demonstrating the CAE’s understanding."
    ],
