      "Considerations for Implementation\nThe strategy should begin with an understanding of the organization’s direction, stakeholder\nexpectations, and the services established in the internal audit charter.\nThe vision describes the desired future state of internal audit. A three-to-five-year horizon is an\nexample in the guidance, not a mandatory duration.\nStrategic objectives translate the vision into achievable targets. Supporting initiatives describe\nthe actions needed to reach those targets.\nUseful development approaches include:\n     Assessing strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats.\n     Comparing current capabilities with the desired future state.\n     Considering competency development.\n     Evaluating technology that could improve effectiveness and efficiency.\n     Incorporating opportunities to improve the function as a whole.\nInitiatives should be prioritized and assigned target dates. Progress can be monitored through\nrelevant performance measures.\nThe strategy should be reconsidered when organizational objectives or stakeholder expectations\nchange. Other review triggers may include changes in leadership, relevant requirements,\ngovernance maturity, or quality assessment results."
