        "number": 1,
        "title": "Standards & Guidance",
        "text": "Requirements\nThe chief audit executive (CAE) must develop and conduct internal assessments of:\n     The internal audit function’s conformance with the Global Internal Audit Standards.\n     Its progress toward performance objectives.\nThe CAE must establish an internal assessment methodology that includes:\n    1. Ongoing monitoring of conformance and progress toward performance objectives.\n    2. Periodic self-assessments, or assessments by other people within the organization who\n         have sufficient knowledge of internal audit practices.\n    3. Communication of internal assessment results to the board and senior management.\nBased on periodic self-assessment results, the CAE must develop action plans addressing\nnonconformance and improvement opportunities, including proposed implementation timelines.\nThe results and action plans must be communicated to the board and senior management.\nInternal assessments must be documented and included in the evaluation performed by the\nindependent third party conducting the external quality assessment.\nIf nonconformance affects the overall scope or operation of the internal audit function, the CAE\nmust disclose both the nonconformance and its impact to the board and senior management.\nConsiderations for Implementation\nA. Ongoing monitoring\nOngoing monitoring is embedded in daily supervision, review, and management of the internal\naudit function.\nActivities may include:\n     Reviewing engagement planning, workpapers, and final communications.\n     Checking application of internal audit methodologies.\n     Using templates, checklists, or automated workpapers to support consistent practice.\n     Obtaining stakeholder feedback on engagement effectiveness and efficiency.\n     Monitoring completion of the audit plan, engagement timeliness, resource allocation, and\n         budget variances.\n     Identifying recurring weaknesses and taking improvement actions.\nSupervision should begin during planning and continue throughout the engagement.\nB. Periodic self-assessments\nPeriodic assessments provide a broader review of the internal audit function and address\nconformance with every standard.\nAssessors may include experienced internal auditors, a dedicated quality team, or other\norganizational personnel with appropriate audit knowledge.\nThe assessment should consider:\n     Adequacy and application of internal audit methodologies.\n     Contribution to organizational objectives.\n     Quality of services and supervision.\n     Achievement of performance objectives.\n     Fulfillment of stakeholder expectations.\nInvolving internal auditors can also develop their understanding of the Standards.\nThe supplied Standard 12.1 requires periodic self-assessments but does not prescribe a\nfixed annual frequency for conducting them. The methodology should define appropriate\narrangements. Reporting requirements should also be read alongside Standard 8.3.\nExamples of Evidence of Conformance\n     Completed workpaper review checklists.\n     Stakeholder survey results and performance measures.\n     Periodic assessment plans, workpapers, and reports.\n     Board and management presentations and meeting minutes.\n     Documented monitoring results and corrective action plans.\n     Evidence of improvements to efficiency, effectiveness, and conformance."
