        "title": "Standards & Guidance",
        "text": "Requirements\nFor every engagement, internal auditors must develop a final communication containing:\n     The engagement objectives.\n     The engagement scope.\n     Recommendations and/or action plans, where applicable.\n     Engagement conclusions.\nFor assurance engagements, the final communication must also contain:\n     The findings, including their significance and prioritization.\n     An explanation of any scope limitations.\n     A conclusion on the effectiveness of the governance, risk management, and control\n         processes of the activity reviewed.\nResponsibility and Completion Dates\nThe final communication must identify the individuals responsible for addressing the findings\nand the planned completion dates for the actions.\nResponsibilities should be presented clearly enough for the recipients to understand who is\naccountable for addressing each finding and when completion is expected.\nActions Taken Before Final Communication\nWhen internal auditors become aware that management has started or completed actions\naddressing a finding before the final communication is issued, those actions must be\nacknowledged.\nAcknowledging an action does not, by itself, establish that internal audit has verified its\nimplementation or effectiveness. The communication should accurately describe the status\nsupported by the available evidence.\nCommunication Quality\nThe final communication must meet the seven characteristics described in Standard 11.2:\nCharacteristic  Application to the final communication\nAccurate        Facts, calculations, references, and descriptions are correct.\nObjective       Results are presented fairly and reflect a balanced evaluation of the evidence.\nClear           Readers can understand the findings, conclusions, and expected actions.\nConcise         Unnecessary repetition and irrelevant detail are removed.\nConstructive The communication supports improvement and appropriate action.\nComplete        Information necessary to understand the results is included.\nTimely          Results reach the appropriate parties in time to support action.\nReview, Approval, and Distribution\nInternal auditors must ensure that the chief audit executive reviews and approves the final\ncommunication before it is issued.\nThe chief audit executive must distribute the final communication to parties who can ensure that\nits results receive appropriate consideration.\nDisclosure of Nonconformance\nIf the engagement was not conducted in conformance with the Standards, the final\ncommunication must disclose:\n    1. The standards with which conformance was not achieved.\n    2. The reasons for the nonconformance.\n    3. The impact on engagement findings and conclusions.\nConsiderations for Implementation\nA statement that the engagement was conducted in conformance with the Global Internal Audit\nStandards should be included when supported by engagement supervision and the quality\nassurance and improvement program.\nA conformance statement should therefore be based on supporting evidence rather than inserted\nautomatically into every report.\nThe chief audit executive may establish templates and procedures for final communications. The\nformat may vary according to the organization and the intended audience.\nDifferent versions may be prepared for different audiences, taking account of:\n     Their knowledge of the activity reviewed.\n     How the findings and conclusions affect them.\n     How they will use the information.\n     The level of detail they need.\nAdditional report components may include a title, background, recognition of positive aspects or\ncooperation, and a distribution list.\nBefore issuance, the review should verify that:\n     The documented work is consistent with the objectives, scope, and Standards.\n     Results are clear and supported by relevant, reliable, and sufficient information.\n     Requirements for communication with management of the activity reviewed have been\n         met.\nOral final communications are usually supported by presentation materials, meeting notes, or a\nwritten report.\nExamples of Evidence of Conformance\n     Written final engagement communications.\n     Presentation slides or meeting notes supporting oral communications.\n     Records demonstrating review and approval before issuance.\n     Documentation showing that communication requirements with the activity reviewed\n         were fulfilled."
